=2E..and I agree that inconsistencies shall be avoided if possible.
But I think there aren't necessarily any in this specific case.

To make my view a bit more apparent by an example; in Nethack
there are physical (acid, ordinary water, fruit, oil, etc.) and
magical potions; they behave differently, e.g., if they are
magically cancelled. That's also a deliberate inconsistency
(physical/magical) with potions; but it's not unreasonable.

A _magic_ blow of fire could directly act on the substance of
the targetted entity without the necessity to physically react
(stream of energy, dehydration of cell water, induction of the
nerves, etc.) the known way. (We have the comfortable situation
that magic is not science.)

While the resistances could be seen as a physical change of the
material (inclusive a human's body) to prevent the interaction
of energy and matter, the magic attack might not require that
"interaction channel" at all, so could be directly effective.

(That all might sound a bit academic - trying to find rational
sounding explanations for irrational magic -, but it's not far
fetched if we accept magic as concept and apply an equivalence
principle in our argumentation.)

